nd  early 80's is collecting  african-american art rare books and  manuscripts and I went to Savannah to  give a talk to high school teachers  which have done many times about  Douglass and my host which was the  Georgia Historical Society said there's  a local gentleman here he's a collector  he'd like to meet you and I said fine  that day I met Walter and Walters  collection is the essential reason I  wrote this new biography I didn't commit  immediately because it was a daunting  task I didn't know if I wanted to take  this on but that collection especially  opened up the last third of Frederick  Douglass's life he's only in his 40s  when the Civil War occurs and the  Emancipation comes but Douglass lives 30  more years to 1895 and lived out in a  remarkable and complicated both public  and family life the core of the Evans  collection are about 10 very large  family scrapbooks that were kept by his  sons plus a lot of family letters and  other kinds of family legal documents  and photographs so that collection gave  me a window like we had never had into  the older Douglass the aging Douglass  the Douglass who becomes the patriarch  of a huge extended family but also the  Douglass who becomes a kind of political  insider in Washington DC and within the  19th century Republican Party so it was  it was access to a third of Douglass's  life we had never seen before and that  collection really is the  I wrote the new biography and I was able  to employ I hope a lot of new insights  about Douglas particularly that latter  part of his life and it's why I  dedicated the book to Walter and Linda  Evans so any anything that surprised you  when you were going through the  materials and what  oh gosh many many things but for example  in these scrapbooks suddenly I happened  upon two handwritten narratives there  are fairly short dozen pages maybe by  two of Douglass's three surviving sons  entitled growing up in the Douglass  household that kind of revealing access  to the