e  1880s not the 1980's the most famous  black man in the country married a white  woman 20 years younger he got pilloried  from the black press the white press  although he got defended as well he was  a defendant by many of his friends his  children however did not take easily to  it it was a very complicated difficult  part of his story  they never warmed up to Helen to put it  nicely although they said all the right  things to the press so this collection  opened up that story to me which could  have been an entire chapter of the book  I make it a part of the chapter it's  those sorts of elements of Douglass's  life I could go on about this there's  the case where his son-in-law rose  as husband who was not a good husband  I'll leave it there but at one point he  sued Douglas his own son-in-law sued him  that's exploded in the press and Douglas  had to settle out of court with his own  son-in-law this sort of stuff led me to  actually use the label in the book that  Douglass's family his extended family  became in effect the blackfurs family in  Washington DC everything they did good  bad ugly got into the press if there was  a bankruptcy for one of the sons it's in  the press he gets sued it's in the press  he gets sure he gets accused of nepotism  for hiring his four children as his  clerks which it was and at one point he  basically he said sure okay it's a  nepotism but my kids need jobs you know  anyway  it allowed me for a biographer what the  Evans collection allowed me was texture  it gives you anecdotal stories that you  can begin to piece together to show the  nature of a life and as we all know  every day of our lives we live a partly  public life and a partly private life  and if you're doing biography you do  both you have to do both and virtually  nothing is off limits in biography  because you're trying to get a sense of  the person the oven's collection got me  into family life private life and not  just you know there's anything salacious  I'm talking about how life was actually  lived for this aging federal bureaucrat  who also is the most famous abolitionist  in the country who is this heroic  symbolic figure always associated with  slavery and emancipation and the victory  of the Civil War who was always  associated with being you know the head  of his race people would even write to  him black people would write to him and  say dear chieftain of the race Wow  it wouldn't even say dear mr. Douglass  he lived with this kind of well we would  call it celebrity today  didn't use that term they called it Fame  he had a serious problem with how to  manage fame and his reputation and then  have the reputation of his whole  extended family because his kids didn't  all succeed they didn't all do so well  and he's constantly sending the money  constantly helping them out in ways that  really weighed on him as an individual  but then there's so many other public  events that he gets associated with and  gets involved in controversies of all  kinds about issues over the Republican  Party issues over the Kansas migration  Exodus issues over every election year  and then eventually he got appointed  u.s. minister to Haiti which meant  ambassador to Haiti in 1889 when he's in  his 70s and that became very  controversial because of the kinds of  policies expansions policies that he was  asked to enforce there and everything  Douglass did in Haiti was all over the  press so I won't say I fell in love with  the older Douglass that's not the way to  put it at all but I became deeply  fascinated with this latter part of his  life where for so many for so long  people have written about Douglass have  always been less interested in that that  this was the aging man who became this  representative man he had a nice big  house in Washington he held these  federal appointments he was always off  lecturing yeah okay and that was never  as interesting as the young heroic  escaped slave who becomes a great orator  the great abol