 in three autobiographies especially the  first two so he is kind of his own guide  for a lot of that although you got to  penetrate that too with some skepticism  writing those chapters was a bit quicker  in part because the source material is  so much  contained the older he gets the more  letters they are the more speeches there  are the more complications that are on  his life the children are growing up  their adults is lots of friendships and  relationships some of which are pretty  conflicted so the older my subject got  the chapters all got longer that was a  problem  and my editor kept trying to pull me  back pulling me back and as you probably  noticed it's a very long book I know and  you know people have asked me what is  your theory of biography I don't know  that yet I have one because you're if I  have a theory of biography it is simply  you tell the story as it was lived you  try to get inside of the life and then  narrate it as it is lived stopping on  and you're making choices all the time  but stopping on the episodes that are  most important and those indeed that you  have the most evidence for and you are  narrating you're telling a story but  you're also trying to recreate a  character a personality a human being  you're trying to help your reader  understand how this person lived and  thought and behaved and you got to say  it when you think this person didn't  behave well or was utterly insensitive  to something or worse but you just kind  of tell it and what I mean by that is I  started out with elaborate outlines was  this 22 chapter outline I just outlined  the whole book and it kept changing it  then changing and I realized finally I  don't know somewhere halfway through I  just throw out the outline and let the  chapters evolve as they do now clearly I  would make a plan that this chapter I'm  going to cover these two years of the  war or this chapter I'm going to cover  these three major episodes during  reconstruction but it kept it always  chronological of course but also it's  still dug it's what's in front of  Douglas what's what's what Douglas is  living and then anybody else who has a  role in that but you know there are no  real theories of biography you tell the  life as it's happening now they're  different kinds of biographies some  biographies are almost entirely public  some biographies are an attempt to tell  all to tell all private life which you  tend to get about novelist and you know  poets the lives of the poets in this  case though my models for this biography  tended to be some of the great literary  biography is because Douglass was such a  writer he wrote millions of words 1,200  pages of autobiography hundreds and  hundreds of the short form political  editorials in his newspaper and then  thousands of speeches almost all of  which we have a text for Wow he wrote  them down so there are millions of words  he's a he's a word master he's a  creature of language so I looked to a  lot of the at least for me my favorite  literary biographies because I'm writing  about a writer at the same time I rang  about a public figure and some writers  are very much public figures so anyway I  guess I'm a biographer now I wanted to  be or not but it's sometimes it kind of  defines itself as you go  I am wondering you know in your first  book Douglas sets out as this radical  outsider okay  you know freed from slavery radical  outsider and then over the course of his  life becomes really a political insider  and as you noted you know was made the  ambassador to Haiti so he explained how  that process happened Wow  it is one of the most important  trajectories of his life and fascinating  trajectories you know what happens to  the old radical outsider always outside  of powered knocking on the doors always  demanding the country live up to its  Creed's and a ferocious radical critic  it's what abolitionists were right  variances the civil war where he was  also a radical critic and then he reaps  victory in the middle of life he's only  in his 40s and it's cause one how many  radicals actually win in their lifetime  and then he has to find new roles new  new functions and he worried he worried  greatly there at the end of the war what  do I do now you know in fact when he  wrote about the end of the war in 65  1865 66 in his last autobiography he  called that chapter vast changes and he  opined in the first paragraph of it with  the famous line from Shakespeare's  Othello  he said well fellas occupation is gone I  am without my community without my  church and in Othello its Othello  expressing the great anxiety that he's  lost his armies lost his status he's  lost everything a fella didn't yet know  he's about to lose his wife and even  kill his wife but that  orrible tragedy but Douglass employs  that moment in a fellow and everything  just loss of all meaning and status well  it turns out he hadn't lost his status  at all he was suddenly under constant  demand as a speaker and he became a real  participant in reconstruction although  he was always frustrated that he  couldn't affect policy as much but where  Douglass found his role is where it had  always been his voice his oratorical  voice and his written voice he found  after the war during reconstruction for  example that he now had access to major  newspapers and major magazines he's no  longer editing his own newspaper which  was always as I would let his own voice  now he could write for the New York  Tribune and the North American review  and the independent these major sort of  progressive journals and magazines and  kind of legitimized him oh yeah legit  amazed him but he was so legitimized as  well as an orator  he's the invited order at the  of the Freedmen's memorial the famous  statue of Lincoln standing and with the  kneeling slave in 1876 where he gave  what I think is the second greatest  speech second only to his fourth of July  speech of 1852 and then with time  Douglass is constantly constantly  pressed and invited to express what does  he think about this what does he think  about that then the Republican Party  finally embraces him and sends him out  on the stump every presidential election  doug has campaigned for every Republican  president from grant to the end in the  1890s not always with a great deal of  Glee he wasn't always happy with what  was happening to the Republican Party as  it moved away from black civil and  political rights and more toward big  business and was paying less attention  by the xviii far less attention by the  1880s to the crisis in the south but he  found his role with his voice it was  always essentially the only weapon  Douglass ever had was his pen and his  voice and he understood that he also  created a new newspaper for awhile for  three years he and his sons edited a new  paper 1870 1873 in Washington called the  new national era is it Douglass and his  sons were the printers and he was the  editor that fell on hard times and he  lost about ten thousand dollars on it  and then he got appointed the president  of the Freedman's Bank in 1874 which  also failed on his watch he admitted he  wasn't a banker it didn't fail because  of him it was already failing when he  took it over so there other he always  called these ventures you know the life  needs ventures and he was always taking  in the next venture excuse me  but the most important venture of his  life was always there in words and by  1881 he writes the third version of his  autobiography called life and times of  Frederick Douglass and one of the things  you have to do if you're his biographer  when you write a biography of someone  who wrote 1,200 pages of autobiography  three times his own version of his life  is always there in front of you the  subject is in the way because he's  already imposing the story on you and  those autobiographies have almost  nothing about his family life his two  marriages his friendships relationships  etc etc it's 