e  1880s not the 1980's the most famous  black man in the country married a white  woman 20 years younger he got pilloried  from the black press the white press  although he got defended as well he was  a defendant by many of his friends his  children however did not take easily to  it it was a very complicated difficult  part of his story  they never warmed up to Helen to put it  nicely although they said all the right  things to the press so this collection  opened up that story to me which could  have been an entire chapter of the book  I make it a part of the chapter it's  those sorts of elements of Douglass's  life I could go on about this there's  the case where his son-in-law rose  as husband who was not a good husband  I'll leave it there but at one point he  sued Douglas his own son-in-law sued him  that's exploded in the press and Douglas  had to settle out of court with his own  son-in-law this sort of stuff led me to  actually use the label in the book that  Douglass's family his extended family  became in effect the blackfurs family in  Washington DC everything they did good  bad ugly got into the press if there was  a bankruptcy for one of the sons it's in  the press he gets sued it's in the press  he gets sure he gets accused of nepotism  for hiring his four children as his  clerks which it was and at one point he  basically he said sure okay it's a  nepotism but my kids need jobs you know  anyway  it allowed me for a biographer what the  Evans collection allowed me was texture  it gives you anecdotal stories that you  can begin to piece together to show the  nature of a life and as we all know  every day of our lives we live a partly  public life and a partly private life  and if you're doing biography you do  both you have to do both and virtually  nothing is off limits in biography  because you're trying to get a sense of  the person the oven's collection got me  into family life private life and not  just you know there's anything salacious  I'm t