ways  chronological of course but also it's  still dug it's what's in front of  Douglas what's what's what Douglas is  living and then anybody else who has a  role in that but you know there are no  real theories of biography you tell the  life as it's happening now they're  different kinds of biographies some  biographies are almost entirely public  some biographies are an attempt to tell  all to tell all private life which you  tend to get about novelist and you know  poets the lives of the poets in this  case though my models for this biography  tended to be some of the great literary  biography is because Douglass was such a  writer he wrote millions of words 1,200  pages of autobiography hundreds and  hundreds of the short form political  editorials in his newspaper and then  thousands of speeches almost all of  which we have a text for Wow he wrote  them down so there are millions of words  he's a he's a word master he's a  creature of language so I looked to a  lot of the at least for me my favorite  literary biographies because I'm writing  about a writer at the same time I rang  about a public figure and some writers  are very much public figures so anyway I  guess I'm a biographer now I wanted to  be or not but it's sometimes it kind of  defines itself as you go  I am wondering you know in your first  book Douglas sets out as this radical  outsider okay  you know freed from slavery radical  outsider and then over the course of his  life becomes really a political insider  and as you noted you know was made the  ambassador to Haiti so he explained how  that process happened Wow  it is one of the most important  trajectories of his life and fascinating  trajectories you know what happens to  the old radical outsider always outside  of powered knocking on the doors always  demanding the country live up to its  Creed's and a ferocious radical critic  it's what abolitionists were right  variances the civil war where he was  also a radical critic and then he reaps  victory in the middle of life he's only  in his 40s and it's cause one how many  radicals actually win in their lifetime  and then he has to find new roles new  new functions and he worried he worried  greatly there at the end of the war what  do I do now you know in fact when he  wrote about the end of the war in 65  1865 66 in his last autobiography he  called that chapter vast changes and he  opined in the first paragraph of it with  the famous line from Shakespeare's  Othello  he said well fellas occupation is gone I  am without my community without my  church and in Othello its Othello  expressing the great anxiety that he's  lost his armies lost his status he's  lost everything a fella didn't yet know  he's about to lose his wife and even  kill his wife but that  orrible tragedy but Douglass employs  that moment in a fellow and everything  just loss of all meaning and status well  it turns out he hadn't lost his status  at all he was suddenly under constant  demand as a speaker and he became a real  participant in reconstruction although  he was always frustrated that he  couldn't affect policy as much but where  Douglass found his role is where it had  always been his voice his oratorical  voice and his written voice he found  after the war during reconstruction for  example that he now had access to major  newspapers and major magazines he's no  longer editing his own newspaper which  was always as I would let his own voice  now he could write for the New York  Tribune and the North American review  and the independent these major sort of  progressive journals and magazines and  kind of legitimized him oh yeah legit  amazed him but he was so legitimized as  well as an orator  he's the invited order at the  of the Freedmen's memorial the famous  statue of Lincoln standing and with the  kneeling slave in 1876 where he gave  what I think is the second greatest  speech second only to his fourth of July  speech of 1852 and then with time  Douglass is constant